WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.687
Hosting configuration is part of application security.

2
00:00:03.687 --> 00:00:05.053
A .

3
00:00:05.053 --> 00:00:15.159
NET application may run locally, behind IIS, on Kestrel, in containers, behind a reverse proxy, in a cloud app service, or in a serverless platform.

4
00:00:15.159 --> 00:00:23.354
Each hosting model has settings that affect public URLs, transport behavior, headers, request handling, and diagnostics.

5
00:00:23.354 --> 00:00:31.684
HTTPS expectations, reverse proxy awareness, forwarded headers, and public URL assumptions should be reviewed defensively.

6
00:00:31.684 --> 00:00:47.185
If the application misunderstands the original scheme, host, or client context, it may generate incorrect redirects, apply cookie settings incorrectly, log misleading information, or make access decisions from the wrong inputs.

7
00:00:47.185 --> 00:00:58.657
Middleware configuration influences authentication, authorization, routing, sessions, cookies, request limits, error handling, CORS, static files, and health endpoints.

8
00:00:58.657 --> 00:01:03.915
The same code can behave differently when middleware options or order change.

9
00:01:03.915 --> 00:01:09.309
Startup and hosting configuration should be reviewed as security-relevant code.

10
00:01:09.309 --> 00:01:17.504
Request size limits, upload limits, timeout settings, and resource-protection values help keep the application reliable.

11
00:01:17.504 --> 00:01:25.357
They prevent accidental or excessive requests from consuming memory, disk, threads, or downstream service capacity.

12
00:01:25.357 --> 00:01:30.136
These settings should match endpoint purpose and environment capacity.

13
00:01:30.136 --> 00:01:31.980
Runtime differences matter.

14
00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:38.740
A setting that is safe under local Kestrel may behave differently behind a proxy or in a container.

15
00:01:38.740 --> 00:01:46.593
A static file path, diagnostic route, or health endpoint may be reachable in one environment and hidden in another.

16
00:01:46.593 --> 00:01:52.534
Release review should verify the deployed runtime behavior, not just local assumptions.

17
00:01:52.534 --> 00:02:00.114
Development middleware, debug endpoints, test routes, and diagnostic pages should not be exposed in production.

18
00:02:00.114 --> 00:02:10.425
If production operators need diagnostics, provide controlled, authorized, and logged mechanisms rather than publishing development tools to real users.
